WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:07.000
Uptake transfers nitrogen from water into tissue.

00:07.000 --> 00:13.000
Internal storage, assimilation and structural growth are different processes.

00:13.000 --> 00:20.000
The three containers are compartments in a budget, not three cells.

00:20.000 --> 00:28.000
The one litre vessel contains 20 micromoles of dissolved nitrogen.

00:28.000 --> 00:36.000
Initially the tissue contains one micromole of reserve nitrogen and ten of organic nitrogen.

00:36.000 --> 00:44.000
Light, carbon and other nutrients are sufficient.

00:44.000 --> 00:51.000
The reserve increases when uptake exceeds assimilation.

00:51.000 --> 00:57.000
This illustrative uptake law responds both to the external concentration and to internal reserve status.

00:57.000 --> 01:04.000
Neither the curve nor reserve filling proves passive diffusion.

01:04.000 --> 01:11.000
The experimenter replaces the water with nitrogen-free medium.

01:11.000 --> 01:17.000
The removed nitrogen is recorded as exported.

01:17.000 --> 01:24.000
External uptake becomes zero; reserve nitrogen is still available inside the tissue.

01:24.000 --> 01:31.000
Assimilation moves nitrogen from the reserve into organic tissue.

01:31.000 --> 01:37.000
Structural biomass continues to increase after uptake has stopped.

01:37.000 --> 01:44.000
This model supplies the carbon, light and other requirements for growth.

01:44.000 --> 01:53.000
Reserve nitrogen first rises, then falls after water replacement.

01:53.000 --> 02:03.000
Structural biomass increases by about 0.81 biomass units after the wash.

02:03.000 --> 02:12.000
The model keeps ten micromoles of structural organic nitrogen per biomass unit; reserve mass is separate.

02:12.000 --> 02:19.000
Compare the same initial structural biomass and external nitrogen concentration, with one or eight micromoles of internal reserve.

02:19.000 --> 02:27.000
The model imposes reserve feedback on uptake.

02:27.000 --> 02:34.000
This is a conditional explanation, not a claim that more severe starvation always increases uptake.

02:34.000 --> 02:43.000
Measure external disappearance, internal nitrogen fractions and biomass separately.

02:43.000 --> 02:51.000
A labelled nitrogen pulse followed by a wash can trace earlier uptake into later organic tissue.

02:51.000 --> 03:00.000
Control light, carbon, phosphorus, water delivery and tissue condition.
